{:edb/db-before-t 2599 :edb/db-after-t 2600 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535912 50 #inst "2026-10-09T10:29:18.842Z" 13194139535912 true] [17592186229140 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374074 13194139535912 true] [17592186229141 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374079 13194139535912 true] [17592186355556 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374078 13194139535912 true] [17592186374074 1017 #edb/ref 17592186374075 13194139535912 true] [17592186374074 1089 "q-334937" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374074 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535912 true] [17592186374074 1100 "Given that $Q(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${Q}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${Q}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374074 1113 #uuid "dd7b44a3-6538-5678-b604-afe7ca773072" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374074 1145 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${Q}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nQ(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{Q}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{1}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+0+2{t}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{′}(t) & =6{t}^{2}(\\frac{2}{3}+2{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}(3{t}^{3}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374075 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374076 13194139535912 true] [17592186374075 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374077 13194139535912 true] [17592186374075 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535912 true] [17592186374075 1041 "field-1" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374075 1104 #edb/ref 17592186374076 13194139535912 true] [17592186374075 1110 #uuid "dc22ace5-3daf-5a2b-b007-a1c8b707bcf3" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374076 1034 "4t^2+12t^5" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374076 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535912 true] [17592186374076 1111 #uuid "7d01aeec-7d6a-5e3c-bc6f-930ea3caafad" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374077 1034 "4t^2+6t^5" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374077 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535912 true] [17592186374077 1111 #uuid "b9fb68e4-e292-53f8-a493-4e4ef07e4a42" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374078 1034 "1+e^t" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374078 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535912 true] [17592186374078 1111 #uuid "670786da-dad7-5799-a1c5-50cf557b21f3" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374079 1017 #edb/ref 17592186374080 13194139535912 true] [17592186374079 1089 "q-335252" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374079 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535912 true] [17592186374079 1100 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{t}^{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374079 1113 #uuid "9642dfed-e959-50ee-bb3a-bb0e4c6f718b" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374079 1145 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{-{t}^{2}x}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-2tx{e}^{-{t}^{2}x},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374080 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374081 13194139535912 true] [17592186374080 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374082 13194139535912 true] [17592186374080 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374083 13194139535912 true] [17592186374080 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374084 13194139535912 true] [17592186374080 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374085 13194139535912 true] [17592186374080 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535912 true] [17592186374080 1041 "selection" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374080 1104 #edb/ref 17592186374082 13194139535912 true] [17592186374080 1110 #uuid "dca1d5cf-effe-5489-9ee3-4eb786f87d58" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374081 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374081 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535912 true] [17592186374081 1111 #uuid "56d65fc7-222b-57df-af18-d5f179328795" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374082 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374082 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535912 true] [17592186374082 1111 #uuid "00c9eb58-0f77-54ed-b99a-75d7ea415170" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374083 1034 "III only" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374083 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535912 true] [17592186374083 1111 #uuid "72547652-05c5-564c-9e68-1ce3e6081bc6" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374084 1034 "None" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374084 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535912 true] [17592186374084 1111 #uuid "52bff97b-d237-5101-b351-f70e38d15ff3" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374085 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535912 true] [17592186374085 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535912 true] [17592186